            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue((New_Ret == true) ? (Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX) : true);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is false, x is not in the array or any index is not changed
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue((New_Ret == false) ? ((Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1) && (New_arrListIndexOfX == -1)) : true); 
        }
    }
}

So when I run the Pex stress test, it generates a test coverage for the ArrayList class. It stresses this method by adding integers to the list, and then checks if the new array list contains the integer or not, and checks the number of elements, the index where the element is first found, and the index where the element is lastly found. 
My question is what does the PexObserve.ValueForViewing do in the above code and how to use it for my problem, especially when I don’t know what to observe or what to do with the data it provides.

A: PexObserve.ValueForViewing is used for supplying values into the Pex's DataLog. This data is then used for viewing during the stress-test runs, but it does not influence the result of your tests (the list.Contains result).
In your case, you can utilize PexObserve.ValueForViewing in the following manner:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

This will log these variables and their values during the test runs. The values will be presented in the DataLog section when Pex is run in interactive mode. It's basically a way to help diagnose why your test is failing, or at least where in the generated test inputs the issue arises. It allows you to easily see the changing values of your variables during tests.
As for your question, "I don’t know what to observe or what to do with the data it provides.", you are right. PexObserve doesn't influence your test results. It mainly provides you with means to understand test failures/errors in detail. Observing values for viewing is generally useful when your tests are failing, especially when they're failing randomly or exhibit unexpected behaviors.
Finally, this function doesn't help you solve your problem directly. It's more a means to make your test run with a better understanding if it's failing.

A: The information provided by PexObserve.ValueForViewing is used for debugging the input domain under test and may be helpful in understanding the behavior of the code if it's failing. However, the return of the test, i.e., list.Contains(x) is the ultimate subject of interest.
That being said, the purpose of PexObserve.ValueForViewing would be to log or document the information related to your test under various conditions, so that in case of test failure, you can check with which particular conditions or input the test failed.
Apart from logging or commenting on these values, you may not need to use these values for checking / improving your code (i.e., ArrayList.Contains function), because of the characteristics of the function; i.e., whether an item is in the ArrayList can be inferred solely by the return value without any need to check other values.
For understanding and in-depth understanding of your code, you should use these methods and tools properly in context to their applications.
That’s the correct usage/insight of PexObserve.ValueForViewing, it isn’t for your requirements but more of a debug tool.
Hope this helps~

