Please note these tests might still fail unless you define what it means for two `ArrayList` instances to be equivalent in terms of their elements, which is undecidable and unprovable without additional constraints or context. This is because two `ArrayList` instances may have different elements, yet appear to be equivalent.

To fix this, you may have to define additional preconditions (`AssumePrecondition`) that cover the possible differences between two equivalent `ArrayList` instances. For example, if the elements in your `ArrayList` are numbers and you care about their relative ordering rather than their exact values, then you could add an Assume like this:

```csharp
AssumePrecondition.ExistInAnyOrder(old_arrList.ToArray(), new_arrList.ToArray());
```

This example is also failing but it is showing how to define those additional assumptions.

This is a way to define the equivalence relation for `ArrayList` instances. But for more complex types, you might need to define a custom equivalence relation.

> This is an excerpt of a test created using Pex. This is not a complete test suite and lacks many important aspects that are crucial while creating an actual test suite.
{.is-warning}

