Here is snapshot of tests execution:

As we can see, the second test case failed, because of it's incorrect assumption. Let's check it now.

Analysis of Test Failure:
As mentioned above, this failure can be attributed to the fact that the PexAssumeUnderTest would have allowed the execution to fail in the first place, indicating an actual incorrect execution of the code.

In this case we see that the list did have the last element in it already, so we add that value to our list in our assumption.

Pex has failed correctly indicating that there was no need to examine this test case as it did not give meaningful results.

Test Case: 'PUT_ContainsContract' [Failed]
  Duration: 44 ms
  Test Result: Pass
  Stack Trace:    at ArrayList.Test.ArrayListContractTest.PUT_ContainsContract(ArrayList arrList, Int32 x) in E:\Projects\ArrayList\ArrayList.Test\ArrayListContractTest.cs:line 59
  Failure Details:
  Assert.IsTrue failed.

As we can see from the stack trace, the assertion is failed and stack trace points to the line 59, this is indeed the location from which we were expecting x to be in the list and I guess that it was trying to add item which is already in the list, that's why the test case was failing.

After checking the original code, no place where we have added the same item to the list intentionally, this could only be a bug. So, we're safe to say that our assumption was incorrect.

P.S -> If your code has any conditions to add elements to the list and you are using Pex, you should always use PexAssumeUnderTest on top of your method.

I hope that helps folks to avoid getting these failures and providing meaningful output with their tests.

Conclusion: Assumptions are incredibly important, and you should always ensure that they are correctly placed in your test cases.


Note: To run such test cases you need to have Nuncil JetBrains Test Adapter installed in Visual Studio.

Hope this clarified the situation!!!

A: ArrayList isn't correctly supported by Pex. It's better to use something that's supported like List, IEnumerable, etc. But if you needed to use ArrayList for testing and Pex, use PexAssumeUnderTest attribute.

[PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute tells Pex that this method should be executed under test, and that the method should not perform any invalid operations.

So, If you think that your ArrayList implementation is working correctly according to its contract, you just use PexAssumeUnderTest.

Refer this for more examples :
https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/how-to-verify-that-code-is-being-tested?view=vs-2019#specifying-the-under-test-and-the-under-not-test-method-pairs

A: ArrayList's implementation is not compatible with Pex, it's recommended to use generic list instead. Here are the steps you can follow:


*

*Convert ArrayList type to List<int>.

*Use [PexMethod] attribute.

*The [PexMethod] attribute tells Pex that it should generate tests for this method.


Your test method should look something like this:
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
[PexMethod]
public void YourTestMethod(List<int> list)
{
....
}

I hope--------------------	    if (New_Ret)
	    {
		    Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX);
	    }

            // If the old array list had x at any index i, the 
            // new array list should also have x at index i or later.
            if (Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            }

            // Invariant to make sure ArrayList doesn't corrupt itself.
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}

```
This is a Pex method contracts test to ensure the the `Contains` method of the `ArrayList` class doesn't corrupt the data in array. The new array list `Count` doesn't decrease. This is an important property for many ArrayList operations to maintain the size integrity.

- `Old_arrListCount` gives the initial count of the array list before the Contains operation.
- `New_arrListCount` gives the count of the array list after the Contains operation.

This will also help to see the `IndexOf` method's operation to observe the effect of the `Contains` call i.e, the return value of the `Contains` operation might increment the index of the element at or after the one being requested.
Lastly, the test case asserts that for every `x` in the `arrayList`, if `ArrayList.Contains(x)` returns true, then `ArrayList.IndexOf(x)` should decrease or stay the same.

This is a test of the `Contains` method's post-conditions, which ensure that the state of the `ArrayList` post-operation doesn't change in a way that would make sense for the state-of-the-art data type.
This also forms a test to verify if the ArrayList operation is working as expected. 

It is worth noting the importance of contract testing as it insures that the function's behaviour continues to be consistent across different calls, preserving the function's state for subsequent calls (without invalidating the state). It ensures correct operation of a component's operation during the execution as well as the post-conditions.

For every value a `Contains` can accept (value to be found in List), there should be a test where `IndexOf` will be greater or equal, and `LastIndexOf` will be always less or equal. It means `Contains` is working as expected and elements are not being lost/duplicated.

Also, the test case ensures that if we add an element which is contained in `ArrayList`, then return value will be true otherwise false. This is a test to check the presence of an element in the `ArrayList`.

In the test, the property of count doesn't decrease ensures that the items in the `ArrayList` are not being deleted or lost in the process of testing the `Contains` method. This is a fundamental part of any data structure which maintains its integrity. 

Last but not least, the test ensures that whenever a call to Contains returns true, IndexOf method should not increase for the same element x. IndexOf method returns a zero-based index of the occurrence of x in List, as per the `Contains` method's rules. 

The overall objective of the test is to verify that the behavior of `ArrayList` methods remains consistent as it performs operations on it.


**Conclusion**

Contracts ensure that even though the inputs change, the outputs of the methods remain consistent and follow the expected constraints. This makes sure that the overall functionality and performance of the software remain intact and helps to identify and debug if any issue happens.

In conclusion, this contract test for `Contains` ensures that the `ArrayList` is maintaining its integrity by enforcing that the behavior remains consistent post an operation. 


